The smallest good-outcome bonus Δ that makes high effort incentive-compatible is 80/3 ≈ 26.666667.

To see this, let B be the base wage and Δ the bonus on a good outcome. The artisan's expected utility from high effort is \(U_H = B + 0.8Δ - 10\) and from low effort is \(U_L = B + 0.5Δ - 2\). The workshop sets the base wage so the participation constraint binds at 0. If high effort is chosen, \(U_H = 0\) gives \(B = 10 - 0.8Δ\). The artisan will choose high effort if \(U_H \ge U_L\), which becomes \(0 \ge (10 - 0.8Δ) + 0.5Δ - 2 = 8 - 0.3Δ\), or \(Δ \ge 80/3\). The same threshold arises if the base is set so \(U_L = 0\) and high effort is required to be optimal. Thus the minimum Δ is 80/3, which to six decimal places is 26.666667.

\boxed{26.666667}
